content.text:
Now fetch all sources in execute and inject. Need to be careful with nested template literals: source code contains backticks? chess.global.js minified might contain template literals? Likely not (ES5 built). To inject safely, build the script with JSON.stringify for each source and concatenate in page: `window.eval(<json string>)`. But `eval` inside evaluate? CSP may block eval on Lichess (script-src may not allow unsafe-eval). DevTools evaluate bypasses CSP, but `eval()` called by page script might be blocked by CSP. Actually CSP `unsafe-eval` on lichess? Let's check: lichess CSP has `script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval' ...`? Lichess uses wasm/stockfish, likely allows wasm-unsafe-eval, not eval. But dynamically creating a `<script>` tag with textContent might be blocked by CSP too (inline scripts not allowed).

Alternative: concatenate the sources directly into the evaluated script string (not eval). `tools.browser.evaluate` runs as a function body via DevTools `Runtime.evaluate`, so CSP doesn't apply. The inline injection we did before worked, so concatenation works.

The outer execute code builds a template string with `${coreSrc}` etc. Sources may contain backticks? If I interpolate into a template literal, backticks in the sources would break parsing in execute's Code Mode. Safer to build via JSON.stringify pieces and `new Function`? Hmm, in execute code, I can do:

```js
const script = `(function(){ ... ` + src1 + ` ... })()`;
```
If src1 contains backticks, it's just concatenated into the JS string `script` at runtime — no parse issue in execute, because the template literal only wraps the fixed parts. So use string concatenation, not interpolation inside template literals. E.g.:

```js
const prelude = "(function(){\n" + stubCode + "\n";
const bundles = [chess, core, adapters, overlay, automove, engineClient, content].join("\n;\n");
const postlude = "\n})()";
const script = prelude + bundles + postlude;
```
But stubCode must be defined as a string; I can write it as a normal quoted string or template literal (it has no backticks if I avoid them). Fine.

